1790371796539458	bones-dev	itc:v3:AWKSZJkkk5JkmZMxAAAAQatWqrq1bWwAAAE3Hw		item.create	bn-274e	{"description":"Umbrella for verification work found during a Kani/Verus/loom exploration of bones (2026-09-25).\n\nRouting summary (property -> tool):\n- CRDT merge laws: reduce to 'wins_over is a strict total order' (single-step property), then proptest or Kani.\n- cache/codec.rs bit-level encoders: Kani (type-bounded loops, bit-heavy; complete proofs).\n- ITC clock laws (clock/itc.rs, clock/ops.rs): Verus spike (recursive trees, unbounded).\n- Multi-replica convergence: bones-sim deterministic simulation with clock skew.\n\nOut of scope, with reasons:\n- loom: lock.rs uses fs2 file locks between processes, not in-process shared memory.\n- Kani/Verus on bones-triage and bones-search: floating-point scoring; proptest is the right tool.\n- unsafe surface (mmap.rs, bones-sqlite-vec FFI): already covered by cargo-fuzz targets in fuzz/.\n\nRule for all children: never report a property as proven until a hand-made mutation of the code under test turns the check red.","kind":"goal","labels":["verification"],"size":"l","title":"Formal verification pass for bones-core"}	blake3:cIGCoegQfSyIsJXxSklu1RGNavLOpMEVkdWW-o6OAaU
1790371848785182	bones-yzou15	itc:v3:AWLJPM5OcyTJJJEAAAAAQdXt13atVUAAAAEzAQ		item.create	bn-t694	{"description":"LwwRegister::compare (crates/bones-core/src/crdt/lww.rs:147) orders writes by ITC causality first, then wall_ts, agent_id, event_hash. Causality is a partial order. When it cannot decide, wall_ts decides. The combination is not transitive, so merge is not associative and can depend on merge order.\n\nCounterexample (confirmed with a scratch crate against bones-core):\n- (x, y) = Stamp::seed().fork()\n- a: x.event() -> stamp_a, wall_ts=3\n- c: x.event() again -> stamp_c (a < c causally), wall_ts=1 (clock skew)\n- b: y.event() -> stamp_b (concurrent with a and c), wall_ts=2\n- a beats b (wall), b beats c (wall), c beats a (causal): a cycle.\n\nFold of the three registers in each order:\n  abc -> c   acb -> b   bac -> c\n  bca -> a   cab -> b   cba -> a\n\nRepro code (main.rs, depends on bones-core by path):\n  let (mut x, mut y) = Stamp::seed().fork();\n  x.event(); let sa = x.clone(); x.event(); let sc = x.clone();\n  y.event(); let sb = y.clone();\n  let a = LwwRegister::new(\"a\".to_string(), sa, 3, \"agent-a\".into(), \"blake3:a\".into());\n  let b = LwwRegister::new(\"b\".to_string(), sb, 2, \"agent-b\".into(), \"blake3:b\".into());\n  let c = LwwRegister::new(\"c\".to_string(), sc, 1, \"agent-c\".into(), \"blake3:c\".into());\n  // merge in all 6 orders, compare .value\n\nWhy tests miss it: tests/generators.rs:68 stamp_from_token and lww_from_token derive stamp AND wall_ts from the same token, so causal order always agrees with wall order.\n\nFix options:\n1. Hybrid-logical-clock discipline: when writing, set wall_ts = max(now, max wall_ts in causal past + 1). Then causal-after implies higher wall_ts and the chain is a total order. Needs a migration story for existing logs with skewed timestamps (a read-side clamp during replay may suffice).\n2. Drop step 1 and compare only (wall_ts, agent_id, event_hash). Simple and total, but a causally later write can lose to an earlier one under skew.\n3. Keep causal step but make ties consistent: hard; any partial-order-first scheme needs the linear-extension guarantee of option 1.\n\nAlso check the same pattern in merge_with_trace (lww.rs:95) and anything else that calls compare.\n\nAcceptance:\n- The independent-generator proptest (sibling bone) fails before the fix and passes after.\n- The 3-register counterexample above converges to one winner in all 6 orders (add as a unit test).\n- Severity note on this bone from the replay-order investigation bone.","kind":"bug","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-274e","size":"m","title":"LWW merge is not associative under clock skew","urgency":"urgent"}	blake3:6M6Zbrbi4hvqMiUyz0H6GNG9lff5EPhfrCL69V9Yuio
1790371848915174	bones-yzou15	itc:v3:AWLJPM5OcyTJJJEAAAAAQdXt13atVUAAAAEzAg		item.create	bn-2fux	{"description":"tests/generators.rs builds LwwRegister values where stamp and wall_ts both derive from one u8 token (stamp_from_token, lww_from_token, line ~68-90). The comment says this is deliberate so leq aligns with LWW assumptions. The result: the semilattice proptests (tests/proptest_semilattice.rs) can never produce a causal order that disagrees with wall order, which hides the non-associativity bug in the sibling LWW bone.\n\nWork:\n- Add a generator that builds a small causal history: fork a seed stamp into 2-4 replicas, apply random event()/join() steps, and pick registers from arbitrary points of that history.\n- Give each register an independently generated wall_ts (small range, e.g. 0..8, so ties and inversions are common), agent_id from a small pool, unique event_hash.\n- Run the existing lww commutative/associative/idempotent laws and the WorkItemState laws against it.\n- Keep the old generator only if something needs it; otherwise replace it.\n\nAcceptance:\n- Against current main, lww_associative fails and proptest shrinks to a 3-register case like the one in the LWW bug bone. Record the shrunk case in a comment on this bone.\n- After the LWW fix, all laws pass at the default 10_000 cases.\n- Seedable via PROPTEST_SEED as today.","kind":"task","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-274e","size":"s","title":"Proptest: generate LWW stamps and wall clocks independently","urgency":"urgent"}	blake3:StELm_74eb2Z1vMuE2CycwHHTezl49BKU8nU84nd1nY
1790371849041549	bones-yzou15	itc:v3:AWLJPM5OcyTJJJEAAAAAQdXt13atVUAAAAEzAw		item.create	bn-24t4	{"description":"The LWW non-associativity bug only causes real divergence if two replicas can apply or merge the same set of writes in different orders. This bone decides the severity of that bug.\n\nTrace:\n- WorkItemState::apply_event (crdt/item_state.rs:214): is it called in a canonical order (sorted by hash? by ITC? by file position?) during replay in db/project.rs and recovery.rs?\n- WorkItemState::merge (item_state.rs:176) and crdt/state.rs:167 merge: which callers merge whole states, and in what order (sync/, sync/prolly.rs, bones-cli git merge_driver.rs, cmd/sync.rs)?\n- Incremental projection: does applying a newly synced event onto an existing projection give the same result as a full rebuild?\n- Note derive_stamp_from_hash fallback (item_state.rs ~226): events without a parsable ITC get a hash-derived stamp. Check whether that can create spurious causal relations.\n\nOutput: a comment on the LWW bug bone stating one of\n- 'reachable: <path>' with a concrete scenario (two replicas, event set, differing result), or\n- 'latent: all paths use canonical order <X>', naming the ordering that protects it.\nEven if latent, the fix still matters because the Merge trait claims semilattice laws.","kind":"task","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-274e","size":"s","title":"Determine whether replay/sync can merge LWW writes in differing orders","urgency":"urgent"}	blake3:Cj45pVEm8QJX8TEROSQ5MflmRNk3skx-hDAEKqe1tss
1790371849169674	bones-yzou15	itc:v3:AWLJPM5OcyTJJJEAAAAAQdXt13atVUAAAAEzBA		item.create	bn-1veg	{"description":"After the LWW fix, prove the single-step property that makes merge a semilattice, instead of proving associativity over sequences.\n\nComposition argument (record it in a comment beside the harness): if wins_over is irreflexive-except-equal, total and transitive on distinct registers, then merge(a,b) = max under that order. max is commutative, associative and idempotent. No induction is needed beyond this.\n\nProperties:\n- totality: for distinct a, b exactly one of a.wins_over(b), b.wins_over(a) holds.\n- transitivity: a wins over b and b wins over c implies a wins over c.\n- self-merge: a.merge(a) == a.\n\nTool choice:\n- Kani if the fix makes the order a plain tuple compare (option 2) or an HLC-clamped wall_ts (option 1): bound the input with small ITC trees (depth <= 2) and short strings. State the bound and its justification in the harness doc.\n- Otherwise a targeted proptest over the independent generator is acceptable. Say which in the bone comment.\n\nAcceptance: harness goes red when the fix is reverted (mutation check), and red when the transitivity assertion is deliberately broken. assert!(false) in the body must fail (no vacuous assumptions).","kind":"task","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-274e","size":"s","title":"Kani/proptest proof: LwwRegister::wins_over is a strict total order"}	blake3:Q9xY8DUJR_IpbN8v8sY5_7HIt3zUMTsvmGycJxvPmI0
1790371849299039	bones-yzou15	itc:v3:AWLJPM5OcyTJJJEAAAAAQdXt13atVUAAAAEzBQ		item.create	bn-16g3	{"description":"crates/bones-core/src/cache/codec.rs:66 decode_varint.\n\n- The loop allows shift up to 63, so a 10th byte is read. At shift 63 only bit 0 of that byte fits into the u64. Bits 1-6 of the 10th byte are silently discarded instead of rejected.\n- Consequence: distinct byte strings decode to the same value (decode is not injective), and corrupted cache data can decode to a plausible value instead of DataCorrupted.\n- The error message says 'more than 9 bytes', but 10 bytes are accepted (correctly, since u64::MAX needs 10). Message is misleading.\n\nFix:\n- When shift == 63, reject if (byte & 0x7F) > 1 or if the continuation bit is set.\n- Optionally reject overlong encodings (a trailing 0x00 group after a continuation), if canonical-only decoding is desired. Decide and document.\n- Update the message to 'more than 10 bytes' / 'overflow in 10th byte'.\n\nAcceptance: unit tests for [0xFF x9, 0x02] -> DataCorrupted, [0xFF x9, 0x01] -> u64::MAX. The Kani codec bone includes an injectivity harness that fails before this fix.","kind":"bug","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-274e","size":"xs","title":"decode_varint accepts non-canonical 10-byte encodings and drops bits"}	blake3:zkMpQi-p9bQOHaeKNFfCH5u_1fFqHPn5aB5FzDVthD8
1790371849430023	bones-yzou15	itc:v3:AWLJPM5OcyTJJJEAAAAAQdXt13atVUAAAAEzBg		item.create	bn-12ta	{"description":"Adopt Kani for the columnar cache codec (crates/bones-core/src/cache/codec.rs). Its loops are bounded by the data type and its code is bit manipulation, which is where Kani (CBMC bit-blasting) gives complete proofs cheaply.\n\nSetup:\n- Add #[cfg(kani)] mod proofs in codec.rs (functions are pub(crate)).\n- Add a 'just kani' recipe; do not add it to 'just check' until runtime is known. Record install steps (cargo install --locked kani-verifier; cargo kani setup) in docs/contributor-guide.md.\n\nHarnesses (each over the full input domain unless noted):\n1. varint round-trip: for any u64 x, decode_varint(encode_varint(x)) == Ok((x, buf.len())) and buf.len() <= 10. #[kani::unwind(11)].\n2. varint decode never panics: any [u8; 11] and any length 0..=11.\n3. varint injectivity / canonical form: if decode(b1) == decode(b2) == Ok((v, n)) then b1[..n] == b2[..n]. Must FAIL before the decode_varint bug fix (sibling bone).\n4. zigzag: zigzag_decode(zigzag_encode(n)) == n for all i64, and the reverse for all u64.\n5. TimestampCodec delta encoding (codec.rs ~101-150): round-trip for 2-3 arbitrary u64 timestamps. Check the delta subtraction for overflow when two timestamps differ by more than i64::MAX.\n6. Nibble packing for event types (~360, ~427): round-trip for arrays of length 0..=5 including odd counts; decode never panics on arbitrary packed bytes with arbitrary count.\n7. RLE encoders (rle_encode_u8/u16/u32, ~697-760): decode(encode(xs)) == xs for bounded-length inputs; run lengths cap correctly at the counter type max.\n\nBounds for 5-7 are small-scope bounds on input length; state each in the harness doc comment with its justification.\n\nAcceptance: every harness goes red under a hand mutation (e.g. drop the 0x80 continuation bit, flip zigzag shift, off-by-one in nibble is_last). Record the mutation used per harness in a bone comment.","kind":"task","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-274e","size":"m","title":"Kani harnesses for cache/codec.rs encoders"}	blake3:ahSKaiPXo89JIT5co9L_ofhP--FKfvm9nwD3Y1sBaSw
1790371849559019	bones-yzou15	itc:v3:AWLJPM5OcyTJJJEAAAAAQdXt13atVUAAAAEzBw		item.create	bn-2fs6	{"description":"The LWW non-associativity bug needs clock skew plus concurrent writes to the same LWW field. Check whether bones-sim (crates/bones-sim: clock.rs, network.rs, oracle.rs, campaign.rs) generates that. If not, extend it so the simulator would have caught the bug without a hand-built counterexample.\n\nWork:\n- Per-agent clock offset and drift in the sim clock, including offsets that make a causally later write carry a lower wall_ts.\n- Campaign scenarios where 2-4 agents update the same field (title, state, parent) concurrently, with partial sync and delivery in varied orders (reorder, duplicate, delay).\n- Oracle: after full delivery, all replicas' projections are identical, and identical to a from-scratch rebuild from the union of events.\n- Seeded and replayable as the existing harness is.\n\nAcceptance: with the LWW fix reverted, a seeded campaign finds divergence and prints the seed. With the fix, a campaign of a fixed seed range passes. Note the seed range and run count in a bone comment.","kind":"task","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-274e","size":"m","title":"bones-sim: inject clock skew and concurrent same-field writes"}	blake3:mn2Nzw_wHlgRKW0LDLKHZUQkM02BJeKUYn0ckzs7p2k
1790371849684317	bones-yzou15	itc:v3:AWLJPM5OcyTJJJEAAAAAQdXt13atVUAAAAEzCA		item.create	bn-226p	{"description":"Timeboxed spike to evaluate Verus on bones using the ITC implementation (crates/bones-core/src/clock/itc.rs, clock/ops.rs). ITC is pure, recursive, has published laws (Almeida, Baquero, Fonte 2008), and existing proptests in tests/itc.rs. That makes it the best Verus candidate in the repo and a low-risk learning target.\n\nLaws to prove, unbounded in tree depth:\n- Id/Event normalize is idempotent and preserves meaning (spec fn giving the interval set / the counter function).\n- fork: the two ids are disjoint and their union equals the original id.\n- join: commutative, associative, idempotent on Event (semilattice), and leq is the induced order.\n- event: s.leq(event(s)) and not event(s).leq(s) for a non-anonymous id.\n- leq is a partial order.\n\nApproach:\n- Start with a verified copy in a separate crate (e.g. crates/bones-itc-verus) to learn the tooling without touching bones-core. If proofs close, decide whether bones-core should depend on the verified implementation.\n- Kani fallback for comparison: same laws with trees bounded to depth 3. Compare effort.\n- Run 'cargo verus focus -- --no-cheating' and list any assume/admit/external_body as trusted surface.\n\nOutput: a bone comment with laws proven, lines of proof vs. code, iterations needed, and a go/no-go recommendation on Verus adoption for bones.\nRule: a verified copy that bones-core does not use proves nothing about bones. The go decision must include a plan to use the verified code.","kind":"task","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-274e","size":"l","title":"Verus spike: prove Interval Tree Clock laws","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:iXDERTdXQQ_trNiMRw_FUkRAFHfnbUc9Hgpb54eGj28
